  <diagram id="clvq-vae-architecture" name="Cross-layer VQ-VAE">
    <mxGraphModel grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1152" pageHeight="480" background="#ffffff" description="Illustrative trace for next, chess and match. Blue bars show lower-layer activations, actual higher-layer targets and their reconstructions. The adaptive residual encoder mixes each lower-layer activation with a learned transformation. Amber bars show its continuous outputs. During training the quantizer samples a code for each token from its top-k candidates. The teal bars show the stored vector for that code. Decoder self-attention uses the sequence of sampled vectors; cross-attention reads the original amber sequence without quantization. The decoder reconstructs the higher-layer states of the same tokens. Bar heights are illustrative coordinates, not measured activations. Adapted from Garg et al., Figure 1 and Section 2." sourceUrl="https://arxiv.org/html/2506.20040v4"><root>
      <mxCell id="0"/><mxCell id="1" parent="0"/>
      <mxCell id="frozen-lm" value="" style="shape=rectangle;rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=16;fillColor=#f7f9fa;strokeColor=#c6d2d9;strokeWidth=1.3;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="20" y="20" width="150" height="416" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
      <mxCell id="frozen-title" value="Frozen LM" style="text;html=0;align=center;verticalAlign=top;resizable=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;whiteSpace=nowrap;overflow=visible;spacing=0;fontFamily=Arial;fontSize=17;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#285f8f;" vertex="1" parent="frozen-lm"><mxGeometry x="29" y="8.719999999999999" width="92" height="23" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
